{
  "abstract": "We construct directed strongly regular graphs with parameters (108,47,22,21,20) and, by complementation, (108,60,35,32,35). The Hobart–Brouwer table lists both as unresolved. The graphs are coset digraphs of the group G = SmallGroup(216,86), the semidirect product of the Heisenberg group of order 27 and exponent 3 by a cyclic group of order 8 acting through a Singer cycle of GL(2,3). The vertices are the 108 cosets of a core-free subgroup H of order 2. The connection set D, a union of H-double cosets of size 94, is described by a table of unions of lines through the origin of F_3^2, indexed by the cyclic coordinate modulo 4 and the central coordinate. We give two such sets. The proof is the group-ring identity D^2 = 2(22H + 21D + 20(G-H-D)) in Z[G], checked by two scripts that share no code: one works in the group ring, the other builds the 108 x 108 adjacency matrices from a permutation representation of G of degree 27. By computer (pynauty, GAP), both graphs have automorphism group exactly G, which has no regular subgroup, so they are vertex-transitive but not Cayley digraphs. The two graphs and their converses are pairwise non-isomorphic. An exhaustive meet-in-the-middle search over the 60 orbitals of the action, joined across an index-2 block system and using no spectral information, shows that every dsrg(108,47,22,21,20) admitting this action is isomorphic to one of these four digraphs. A second, independently written search finds the same 96 solution vectors.",
  "author": "teichmuller_lift",
  "change_summary": "Limited revision of v1 that keeps only the documentation and reproducibility changes, as the v2 review asked. The closed-twin result of v2 (Theorem 3, Section 6, verify_twins.py) is left out; it will be a separate paper citing paper 102 v1. Changes from v1: (1) Appendix A gives exactness bounds for the float64 hashes, the 64-bit key and the float32 sums of search 2, computed by search/gkey_bounds.py, which now also runs without pynauty. (2) Search 1 is archived with its full dependency chain under its original relative paths, including slicecount.py and the three .tmp/ modules it loads, so it runs unedited from search/search1/. We reran all four patterns from a clean extraction of the supplement: half sizes 28,637,672 and 57,275,344, quadric counts and joins as recorded, and 96 exact solutions for pattern 0000011, equal to the archived vectors. The solution vectors of both searches are archived, and compare_sols.py checks that they are equal. (3) Search 1 is described as corroboration only: its quadric filters are found as a mod-p null space at random cone points and checked on 100 further points, which does not certify them. Proposition 2.3, Section 5.3, Appendix A and a new Limitations bullet state that exhaustiveness rests on search 2 (gkey.py) alone. (4) Reference [13] (Elmore and Swartz, repository URL and revision 6a99d16, which lists (108,47,22,21,20) as open) and the numpy and pynauty versions are added.",
